#!/bin/sh
# script/shell-check: boot the interactive system (`--features shell`) and type at the prompt.
#
#   script/shell-check                     both architectures
#   script/shell-check --arch aarch64      one leg
#   script/shell-check --graphical         both architectures, GPU and keyboard attached
#   script/shell-check --graphical-serial  both architectures, GPU only, typing over the UART
#
# `--graphical` (milestone 177, option A) boots the same `--features shell` kernel with a virtio-gpu
# and a virtio-keyboard instead of the plain UART pair, and verifies it by reading the screen back
# (a QEMU screendump decoded against the same 7x8 font the guest drew from) rather than by piping a
# serial transcript: there is no UART client on that path to pipe. It proves a working, typeable
# prompt reached the screen and a real keystroke round-tripped through `keyboard_driver`'s direct `CALL` to
# `line_editor`; it does not run the full console leg's script (echo, redirects, `wc <`, ...), which
# is real, scoped-out follow-on work. See `shell_check_leg_graphical`'s own doc in `xtask/src/main.rs`.
#
# `--graphical-serial` (milestone 192, option A) is the same boot with the keyboard left off, so the
# keystroke source is the guest's own UART. That is the configuration all three target boards have:
# argon, radon and xenon each have a serial line and none has a virtio-input device, so this is the
# closest QEMU can stand to what a real board will do. Both legs make the same two assertions and
# neither can tell which program typed, which is the property milestone 192's option A is finished
# by. It is NOT a substitute for a bench run: QEMU has neither board's framebuffer handoff
# (milestone 157) nor its real UART timing.
#
# This is the gate for `user/src/system_initializer.rs`. Every other test that runs the shell has
# the KERNEL play init, so a broken init fails nothing; the interactive boot is the only thing that
# runs the real one, and until this existed nothing ran the interactive boot. Milestone 50 hit three
# bugs that way and all three printed nothing at all. See notes/pipes.md.
#
# It is not part of script/test, because it builds a second kernel (`--features shell`) and boots it
# twice. It IS part of `script/gates` and of CI's `script/ci-build`, since milestone 230, and the
# reason is what happened while it was in neither: it went red on `main` on both architectures on
# 2026-08-28 and stayed red for five days, through every green check, because nothing ran it. A
# gate that exists, fails, and tells nobody is worse than no gate, and this one guards the only
# program in the tree that no other test executes.
#
# Both places rather than one. `script/gates` is what a person runs before pushing and pays 30
# seconds against an already-warm target directory; CI's copy is appended to `script/ci-build`
# rather than given a job, so it reuses that job's build instead of paying ten minutes for thirty
# seconds of checking. Run it directly when you touch init, the shell, or what the boot grants them.
#
# BUGS
#
# **The transcript is written by two processes at once and can arrive shuffled.** The kernel prints
# its user-fault reports with its own UART driver; the userspace `console` server drives the same
# device from another address space; nothing arbitrates. So a thread dying while init prints
# splices the two lines together at byte granularity, nondeterministically: milestone 230 watched
# the same commit pass one CI run and fail the next, and watched `construction budget dropped` come
# out with a longest surviving run of five characters. That is a defect in the system rather than in
# this script, it has consequences well past this gate (anything reading a board's serial log), and
# it is proposed as its own milestone in `design/roadmap/230-shell-check-is-red.md`.
#
# What this script does about it: the two boot-line checks put their teeth on the sentence init
# prints when the answer is NO, searched exactly, because interleaving can destroy a string and
# cannot create one. A line that cannot be read at all is reported rather than failed, but only when
# the kernel demonstrably wrote during the boot; with one writer there is nothing to shuffle, so a
# missing line is a real one and still fails. See `boot_claim` in `xtask/src/main.rs`.
#
# **The cost of that choice, stated plainly: it can pass a boot it should have failed.** If init's
# report were deleted AND a thread faulted in the same boot, this reads the second as an explanation
# for the first. Both halves have to happen together and the second is itself a defect the
# transcript shows. The trade is deliberate: a false red taxes every lane whose change had nothing
# to do with it, and a false green here is undone by repetition, since an init that stops dropping
# its budget prints the failing sentence on every boot of both legs on every push.
#
# **A killed user thread is not itself a failure here**, which is how `login` dying at `_start` on
# every boot went unnoticed through passing runs (`user/src/login.rs`'s own BUGS). That assertion is
# the obvious next ratchet and belongs with the fix for it.
#
# **The two graphical legs are red** behind milestone 177's display driver bug, and neither
# `script/gates` nor CI runs them.
#
# Name: unrecorded, and audibly collided with. It boots the kernel's `--features shell`
# configuration and types at the prompt; `shellcheck`, the shell linter this tree runs inside
# `script/lint`, is one hyphen away. The boot mode it names is itself a stale word, since
# notes/naming.md's BUGS records that `shell` no longer names any program (the shell is `swish`).
# Introduced 2026-08-02.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
exec cargo xtask shell-check "$@"
